# GitHub CI/CD + Client Demo Deployment

This deployment setup intentionally leaves the existing chatbot Python/business
logic unchanged.

## Recommended architecture

GitHub repository
    |
    +-- GitHub Actions
    |      |
    |      +-- Compile Python
    |      +-- Run Streamlit smoke tests
    |      +-- Run pytest
    |      +-- Deploy static client showcase to GitHub Pages
    |
    +-- Streamlit Community Cloud
           |
           +-- Runs app.py
           +-- Reads requirements.txt
           +-- Reads OPENROUTER_API_KEY and HF_TOKEN from Streamlit Secrets
           +-- Automatically updates after GitHub commits

GitHub Pages cannot run the Python/Streamlit backend. It is used as the
client-facing project/showcase page. The "Launch Live Demo" button opens the
actual Streamlit app.

## Files to add to the existing repository

Copy these files/folders to the root of your existing project:

.github/
  workflows/
    cicd.yml

docs/
  index.html
  styles.css

GitHub_CICD_Connection_readme.me

No Python source file must be replaced for this deployment setup.

## Step 1 - Push your project to GitHub

From your project folder:

    git init
    git add .
    git commit -m "Add AI market intelligence chatbot and CI/CD"

Create an empty GitHub repository, then:

    git branch -M main
    git remote add origin https://github.com/<YOUR_USERNAME>/<YOUR_REPOSITORY>.git
    git push -u origin main

If `origin` already exists, do not add it again.

## Step 2 - Check GitHub Actions

Open:

    GitHub repository
      -> Actions

Workflow:

    AI Market Intelligence CI/CD

For each push to `main`, CI performs:

1. Checkout repository.
2. Configure Python 3.11.
3. Compile `app.py` and `src/`.
4. Run Streamlit smoke testing.
5. Run your pytest tests.
6. If CI succeeds, package and deploy `/docs` to GitHub Pages.

Pull requests run CI checks but do not publish GitHub Pages.

## Step 3 - Deploy the actual chatbot to Streamlit Community Cloud

Sign in to Streamlit Community Cloud with GitHub.

Create an app and select:

    Repository: <YOUR_USERNAME>/<YOUR_REPOSITORY>
    Branch: main
    Main file path: app.py
    Python: 3.11

In Advanced settings -> Secrets, add:

    OPENROUTER_API_KEY = "your_openrouter_key"
    HF_TOKEN = "your_huggingface_token"

Do NOT commit these values to GitHub.

After deployment, Streamlit gives you a URL similar to:

    https://your-project-name.streamlit.app

Open the URL and validate your chatbot.

## Step 4 - Connect the live demo URL to GitHub Pages

Copy the Streamlit URL.

In GitHub go to:

    Repository
      -> Settings
      -> Secrets and variables
      -> Actions
      -> Variables
      -> New repository variable

Create:

    Name:
    STREAMLIT_DEMO_URL

    Value:
    https://your-project-name.streamlit.app

This is a repository variable, not a secret, because it is a public demo URL.

## Step 5 - Enable GitHub Pages

Go to:

    Repository
      -> Settings
      -> Pages

Under "Build and deployment", select:

    Source: GitHub Actions

Then push a small commit or manually run:

    Actions
      -> AI Market Intelligence CI/CD
      -> Run workflow

After successful deployment, GitHub shows your Pages URL, generally:

    https://<YOUR_USERNAME>.github.io/<YOUR_REPOSITORY>/

Give this URL to clients as the project showcase page.

The "Launch Live Demo" button will send clients to the Streamlit chatbot.

## Step 6 - Normal CI/CD process after setup

Make code change locally:

    git add .
    git commit -m "Update market intelligence chatbot"
    git push origin main

Then automatically:

    Push to GitHub
        |
        v
    GitHub CI validation
        |
        +-- Failure -> deployment stops
        |
        +-- Success
              |
              +-- GitHub Pages showcase is updated
              |
              +-- Streamlit Community Cloud detects the GitHub commit
                  and updates the live application

## Security checklist

Never commit:

    .env
    .streamlit/secrets.toml
    OPENROUTER_API_KEY
    HF_TOKEN

Keep `.env` ignored by Git.

For Streamlit Community Cloud, enter credentials in the Cloud Secrets UI.

If your repository is public, remember that all committed source code and demo
data are visible to anyone. Keep the repository limited to public company facts
and synthetic/demo model parameters; never commit proprietary client data.

## Client demo checklist

Before sharing the Pages URL with a client:

1. GitHub Actions is green.
2. Streamlit live app opens correctly.
3. Both OpenRouter and Hugging Face tokens are configured in Streamlit Secrets.
4. Sidebar displays token check succeeded.
5. Upload and URL news ingestion work.
6. Company-specific analysis returns only that company.
7. Direct master-data questions return only the requested value.
8. Impact reasons remain within the configured word range.
9. Evaluation score is visible.
10. No secret keys appear in GitHub source or logs.

## Important

GitHub Pages is a static web-hosting service. It cannot execute `app.py`.
Therefore, do not attempt to run Streamlit directly in GitHub Pages.

The live application is hosted on Streamlit Community Cloud from the GitHub
repository, while GitHub Pages provides the professional client-facing entry
page.

## Multiple company exposure files

The current application can load multiple CSV/XLSX workbooks from the same setting:

    paths:
      company_exposure_csv:
        - data/company_exposure_2026_part1.xlsx
        - data/company_exposure_2026_part2.xlsx

The two files are combined at application startup and exposed as one company-master dataset.
After adding or removing an exposure file, commit the file plus config/settings.yaml and restart/redeploy the Streamlit app.
